WEBVTT

00:00:03.667 --> 00:00:08.955
In this film, a real Codex client uses Open Reports through the Model Context Protocol.

00:00:08.955 --> 00:00:14.181
It starts with an invoice, makes a small customer-ready design, checks the report, and

00:00:14.181 --> 00:00:19.286
renders the PDF. Then we open the same report in the visual designer and inspect the

00:00:19.286 --> 00:00:20.380
finished document.

00:00:22.416 --> 00:00:26.216
This is the Codex command line running in a real terminal.

00:00:26.216 --> 00:00:30.278
For the film, its MCP configuration enables only Open Reports.

00:00:30.278 --> 00:00:36.108
Codex can inspect report examples, make validated JSON patches, preview pages, and render

00:00:36.108 --> 00:00:42.005
output. Its shell access is read-only, and the other personal MCP servers are disabled for

00:00:42.005 --> 00:00:42.856
this session.

00:00:44.682 --> 00:00:50.231
Codex has completed the tool sequence. I am opening the exact JSON output in the designer

00:00:50.231 --> 00:00:53.411
now, then I will compare it with the generated PDF.

00:00:58.989 --> 00:01:03.908
The visual designer now opens the exact report JSON produced in the Codex workflow.

00:01:03.908 --> 00:01:08.945
The report remains editable: the structure tree shows its sections and table, and the

00:01:08.945 --> 00:01:14.220
canvas shows the invoice on an A4 page. The local engine connection is configured, so the

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.183
designer confirms there are no errors or warnings.

00:01:19.568 --> 00:01:24.375
Run Preview sends the report through the rendering engine and shows the printable result.

00:01:24.375 --> 00:01:28.965
This is useful for checking the actual output before handing the PDF to a customer or

00:01:28.965 --> 00:01:29.397
printer.

00:01:34.441 --> 00:01:38.838
Here is the actual PDF file rendered from that report definition.

00:01:38.838 --> 00:01:44.589
The line items, tax, total, QR payment block, and page footer all fit on one A4 page.

00:01:44.589 --> 00:01:50.407
The same API can return these PDF bytes directly to a host application for printing or

00:01:50.407 --> 00:01:51.016
download.

00:01:55.136 --> 00:02:00.256
The key idea is simple. An AI client can use Open Reports tools to prepare and check a

00:02:00.256 --> 00:02:04.781
report. Your application can keep the JSON definition and its customer data.

00:02:04.781 --> 00:02:08.234
The engine renders the PDF when your application needs it.

00:02:08.234 --> 00:02:12.819
Try the Open Reports demo and integration guide to connect your own workflow.
